{"custom_id": "76ab79c5591a84c3220d6eda50de362fd3778267006912ad", "meta": {"loop": "topk4L06", "task": "infer_discrepancy_patterns_1", "occurrence": 0, "attempt": 0, "max_tokens": 128000, "prompt_sha256": "5194001e5e308a7d89db13828780a6992f59c13cf1f5a75777236da3c24644a3", "prompt_chars": 11324}, "batch_id": "msgbatch_017vuPQBMXMbU5QJdLT6sNog", "saved_utc": "2026-10-23T10:36:10+00:00", "message": {"id": "msg_011CgK1jZ8cTZysAifmfEGj3", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "I'm looking at the two missed disease mentions - \"unilateral retinal telangiectasis\" and \"retinal telangiectasis\" - both functioning as alternative names for Coats' disease that the gold annotations captured but the LLM output missed.\n\nThen there's \"deficiency in G6PD,\" where the disease term gets split by a preposition linking the head noun \"deficiency\" to the enzyme name - a pattern where the actual disorder designation is inverted around \"in.\" And \"Absence of the seventh component of complement\" is another case where a deficiency disease (complement C7 deficiency) gets expressed descriptively rather than as a standard named term, which gold still tags as SpecificDisease.\n\nThen \"fever\" stands out as odd - gold labels it SpecificDisease even though it reads as a symptom in context (\"presenting with fever, arthritis, and cutaneous vasculitis\"), likely annotation noise treating a sign as if it were a disease. The common thread across these cases is that disease mentions take non-canonical surface forms rather than standard disease-suffixed terminology.\n\nI'm also noticing something odd: \"Coats telangiectasis\" shows up as a true positive, anchored by \"Coats disease\" positionally nearby, which complicates my theory about lesion-terms like \"telangiectasis\" never functioning as disease heads.\n\nSo the real distinguishing feature seems to be the eponym — \"Coats\" is a proper-noun disease anchor, whereas \"retinal telangiectasis\" is just a generic anatomical modifier plus lesion term. Similarly, canonical phrasing like \"G6PD deficiency\" or \"glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency\" gets tagged as true positive, while the reversed/split construction \"deficiency in G6PD\" doesn't.\n\nI'm also noticing other periphrastic constructions like \"absence of the seventh component of complement\" or bare symptom nouns like \"fever\" lack a proper disease-nomenclature head. This points toward a single boolean distinguishing feature: whether the mention's head noun is a recognized disease-nomenclature term (disease/syndrome/eponym) versus a generic, descriptive, or symptom-level phrase that encodes the disease concept periphrastically rather than through conventional naming.\n\nTesting this against the \"so-called retinal telangiectasis\" and parenthetical alternative-name cases, though, complicates things—these are synonym/apposition constructions where the disease is named elsewhere too, yet similar synonym mentions like \"Coats telangiectasis\" were correctly labeled as true positives. So appositive or alternate-naming structure alone doesn't fully explain the discrepancy pattern.\n\nWhat stands out more consistently is that the discrepant mentions tend to have descriptive, finding-like heads—\"telangiectasis,\" \"absence,\" \"deficiency,\" \"fever\"—that read as clinical states or symptoms rather than named disease entities, whereas the correctly-labeled ones use disease-type heads like \"disease,\" \"syndrome,\" or premodified constructions like \"G6PD deficiency.\" The key syntactic contrast seems to be premodifier-gene-noun forms versus noun-postmodified-by-PP forms, which may be driving how the model distinguishes states from diseases.\n\nDescriptive/Periphrastic Disease Reference\" — the model anchors SpecificDisease recognition on canonical nomenclature (eponym or proper-name compound plus disease/syndrome/deficiency head) and misses disease concepts realized as compositional phrases: generic-adjective + finding noun, PP-postmodified state nouns, or bare symptom nouns like \"fever.\" I should also flag that \"fever\" is really a symptom rather than a disease, so its gold label may reflect genuine annotation noise rather than a true miss.\n\nChecking against the TP set, symptoms like \"mental retardation\" are consistently tagged DiseaseClass rather than SpecificDisease, which makes the SpecificDisease label on \"fever\" inconsistent with that pattern and likely an annotation boundary issue — so the compositional-reference rule should still explain four of the five cases, treating \"fever\" as the outlier exception.\n\nI also notice the \"deficiency in G6PD\" span includes the prepositional phrase as a boundary choice, and the model correctly left it unlabeled. Separately, naming constructions like \"so-called retinal telangiectasis\" and parenthetical alt-names (\"Coats' disease... (unilateral retinal telangiectasis)\") hint at a secondary cue — metalinguistic naming markers (so-called, parentheses) — though this seems more like a contributing signal than the primary trigger.\n\nSince the output format requires exactly one Pattern Insight, I should consolidate: mention the compositional/periphrastic form as primary, with \"so-called\"/parenthetical framing as secondary cues. I'm also pulling out strong minimal pairs within the same document — \"Coats telangiectasis\" (labeled) vs \"retinal telangiectasis\" (unlabeled), and \"G6PD deficiency\" (labeled) vs \"deficiency in G6PD\" (unlabeled) — both showing that premodification by a specific name triggers labeling while postmodification doesn't.\n\nHowever, testing this against \"deficiency of norrin,\" which is correctly unlabeled despite similar structure, shows surface syntax alone isn't sufficient — the real distinguishing factor is whether the phrase corefers to an already-named disease in the document. I'm formulating the rule around that: label as SpecificDisease when a finding/state noun is either a lexicalized eponym/gene-compound, or an alternate name coreferring to an established disease via appositive/parenthetical framing, otherwise treat it as a generic descriptive state. I'm also flagging \"fever\" as likely annotation inconsistency rather than something to build a rule around, then assembling everything into the required single markdown Pattern Insight with its four fields.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "## Step 1: Deep-Dive Analysis\n\n**Contrast.** The discrepancies and the true positives share the same head nouns. Two discrepancies use *telangiectasis* and one uses *deficiency*, and true positives also contain *telangiectasis* and *deficiency*. The separating feature is **how the disease head is modified**:\n\n- **True positives** use a lexicalized name: a proper-name or gene/enzyme identifier as premodifier, plus a disease-class head. Examples: \"Coats telangiectasis,\" \"G6PD deficiency,\" \"Coats disease,\" \"Norrie disease.\"\n- **Discrepancies** use a compositional description: a generic anatomical or quantity adjective, a prepositional complement, or a bare sign noun. Examples: \"retinal telangiectasis,\" \"deficiency in G6PD,\" \"Absence of the seventh component of complement,\" \"fever.\"\n\n**Isolate the trigger.** The model sees a generic finding or state noun with no disease-nomenclature anchor and reads it as clinical description, so it assigns O.\n\n**Minimal pairs from the same documents:**\n- *10484772:* \"Coats telangiectasis\" is SpecificDisease, but \"retinal telangiectasis\" in the same abstract is missed.\n- *1303173:* \"G6PD deficiency\" is SpecificDisease, but \"deficiency in G6PD\" in the same abstract is missed.\n\n**Hypothesis check.**\n- Examples 1 and 2 are parenthetical or \"so-called\" alternate names for Coats disease, which is already labeled. This is a naming frame, so the phrase is a synonym of an established disease.\n- Example 3 is the same G6PD deficiency referent expressed with a postmodifier.\n- Example 4 is a periphrastic description of C7 deficiency, a named disease.\n- Example 5 (\"fever\") is a sign-level noun. In this schema, the true positives label symptoms such as \"mental retardation\" as DiseaseClass, so this gold label looks inconsistent. I treat it as likely annotation noise rather than part of the pattern.\n- **Counterexample:** \"a deficiency of norrin\" in 10484772 is O in gold. The syntax matches example 3, so syntax alone cannot decide. What separates them is whether the phrase names an established disease (G6PD deficiency) or describes a molecular state (norrin deficiency). The disambiguation therefore depends on coreference with a disease name already present in the document.\n\n## Step 2: Pattern Insight\n\n- **Pattern Name:** Compositional or Periphrastic Disease Mentions Missed as Generic Findings\n- **Confusion Trigger:** A generic finding or state head noun (*telangiectasis*, *deficiency*, *absence*, *fever*) without a disease-nomenclature anchor. It is modified by an anatomical or generic adjective (\"retinal\"), a prepositional complement (\"in G6PD,\" \"of the seventh component of complement\"), or stands alone as a sign. The model reads it as clinical description rather than a disease name.\n- **Contrastive Evidence:** The true positives are lexicalized compounds whose premodifier is a proper name or gene/enzyme identifier (\"Coats telangiectasis,\" \"G6PD deficiency\"). Within the same documents, the identical head noun is labeled SpecificDisease when premodified this way and missed when it is modified by a generic adjective or a PP.\n- **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Label a finding or state noun phrase as SpecificDisease when it functions as a *name* for a disease:\n  1. **Lexicalized compound:** [eponym or gene/enzyme identifier] + [finding or state head] (e.g., \"Coats telangiectasis,\" \"G6PD deficiency\").\n  2. **Coreferential alternate name:** the phrase re-labels a disease already named in the document, signaled by parentheses, appositive glosses, or \"so-called\" (e.g., \"Coats' disease of the retina (unilateral retinal telangiectasis),\" \"so-called retinal telangiectasis\").\n  3. **Periphrastic deficiency disorder:** \"deficiency/absence in/of [gene, enzyme, or component]\" whose referent is an established deficiency disorder (e.g., \"deficiency in G6PD\" = G6PD deficiency).\n\n  Keep O for generic anatomical or quantity modifiers that lack name function, for molecular state descriptions such as \"deficiency of norrin,\" and for bare sign nouns such as \"fever,\" which should be flagged for annotation review rather than forced into this rule.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4440, "output_tokens": 10256, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 8741}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}